From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] windows.ui: input pane, user interaction mode and IInspectable answers

InputPane was a set of stubs: Showing/Hiding and OccludedRect returned
E_NOTIMPL, TryShow always said no and TryHide left its result unset; the
window's pane was handed out as IInputPane whatever interface was asked
for (IInputPane2 callers called the wrong methods).
UIViewSettings.UserInteractionMode was E_NOTIMPL, and many objects'
GetIids, GetRuntimeClassName and GetTrustLevel were too -- C#/WinRT asks
for the runtime class name to wrap objects.

- InputPane is the on-screen keyboard (the system's osk.exe, an
  OSKMainClass window): TryShow starts it or brings it back, TryHide
  minimizes it, OccludedRect is its rectangle in view pixels while it
  shows (empty otherwise), and Showing/Hiding are raised, with the
  rectangle, when it appears and goes (window events, watched on a thread
  of their own while handlers are registered). GetForWindow,
  GetForCurrentView and GetForUIContext return the interface asked for.
- UserInteractionMode is Touch while tablet mode is on (the
  ImmersiveShell TabletMode value), Mouse otherwise.
- GetIids/GetRuntimeClassName/GetTrustLevel for UIViewSettings, InputPane,
  the CoreWindow, UISettings and toast factories, toast notifications,
  notifiers, history and event arguments, and the XML DOM nodes, node
  lists, iterators and attribute maps.

Wine's windows.ui uisettings tests: 42 tests, the same one failure as
before.

Gate: test/inputpane-gate.sh (Xvfb, test/inputpane-probe.c).
Mutants SG_MUTANT_PANE_NEVER_SHOWS (windows.ui/inputpane.c),
SG_MUTANT_ALWAYS_MOUSE (windows.ui/uiviewsettings.c).
